          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            fibrations enjoy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback+stability"} pullback stability
            and cofibrations are stable under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
          %p
            Put differently, in a proper model category,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
            and/or pushouts can be computed with less need for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+replacement"} fibrant and/or cofibrant replacement
          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              is called
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %strong right proper
                  if
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
                  are preserved by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                  along
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrations"} fibrations
              %li
                %p
                  %strong left proper
                  if weak equivalence are preserved by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
                  along cofibrations,
              %li
                %p
                  %strong proper
                  if it is both left and right proper.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              More in detail this means the following. A model category is right proper if for every weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi Mor
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W\subset Mor(C)
              and every fibration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h : C \to B
              the pullback
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi h
                      %mo *
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^* f : A \times_B C \to C
              in
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %msub
                              %mo ×
                              %mi B
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ⇒
                                  %msup
                                    %mi h
                                    %mo *
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi W
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi W
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mrow
                                %mi h
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi F
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A \times_B C &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\;\;\big\downarrow{}^{\mathrlap{\Rightarrow h^* f \in W}}
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\big\downarrow{}^{\mathrlap{f \in W}}
                    \\\
                    C &amp;\underset{h \in F}{\longrightarrow}&amp; B
                    }
            %p is a weak equivalence.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The above definition is the way it is usually phrased, but in fact it is equivalent to a seemingly weaker condition that is sometimes easier to check: for right properness it suffices to assume that weak equivalences are preserved by pullback along fibrations
              = succeed "." do
                %em between fibrant objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              (hence also
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FibrationsBetweenFibrantObjectsSuffice"}
              below.
          %h2#the_rezk_criterion_for_properness The Rezk criterion for properness
          %p
            The following criterion shows that the notion of left or right properness only depends on the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+category"} relative category
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            in the statement below can be replaced by the notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dwyer%E2%80%93Kan+equivalence"} Dwyer–Kan equivalence
            of underlying
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+categories"} relative categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+categories"} homotopy categories
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#Rezk02"}> Rezk
              , Proposition 2.7 (arXiv), Proposition 2.5 (journal).) A model category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is left proper if and only if for every weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X\to Y
              the induced
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⇆
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X/M\leftrightarrows Y/M
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is right proper if and only if for every weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon X\to Y
              the induced
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⇆
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M/X\leftrightarrows M/Y
            %p
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
          %h2#Examples Examples
          %h3#left_proper_model_categories Left proper model categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                by cor.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AllObjectsFibrantImpliesRightProper"}
              %p this includes notably
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
                %li
                  %p
                    the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+quasi-categories"} model structure for quasi-categories
              %p and many model structures derived from these, such as
              %ul
                %li
                  the injective global
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
                  over any (simplicial) category;
            %li
              %p
                the left
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization"} Bousfield localization
                of every left proper
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+model+category"} combinatorial model category
                at a set of morphisms is again left proper.
              %p
                So in particular also the
                %em local
                injective model structures on simplicial presheaves over a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
                are left proper.
          %h3#nonleft_proper_model_categories Non-left proper model categories
          %p
            A class of model structures which tends to be
            %em not
            left proper are model structures on categories of not-necessarily commutative algebras.
          %p For instance
          %ul
            %li
              the standard model structure on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+unital+algebra"} associative unital algebra
              %em not
              left proper (see
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Rezk02"} Rezk
          %p
            But it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalent
            to a model structure that
            %em is
            left proper. This is discussed
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ProperEquivModels"} below
          %h3#right_proper_model_categories Right proper model categories
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                by cor.
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AllObjectsFibrantImpliesRightProper"}
                every model category in which each object is fibrant is right proper.
              %p
                This includes for instance the standard
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} Quillen model structure on topological spaces
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
                is (automatically left proper, since all objects are cofibrant, but also) right proper, even though not all objects are fibrant. See
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets#Properness"} there
          %h3#nonright_proper_model_categories Non-right proper model categories
          %ul
            %li
              “Non-algebraic” models for higher categories (other than higher groupoids) are generally not right proper. For example, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+complete+Segal+spaces"} model structure for complete Segal spaces
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+quasi-categories"} model structure for quasi-categories
              are not right proper (see
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/40938/is-the-model-category-of-complete-segal-spaces-right-proper"} mathoverflow
          %h3#proper_model_categories Proper model categories
          %p Model categories which are both left and right proper include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"}> Top
                \:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
                (
                %a{:href => "classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces#Properness"}> prop.
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"}> sSet
                \:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
                (
                %a{:href => "classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets#Properness"}> here
                )
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em global
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
                and any local such model structure over a site with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/point+of+a+topos"} enough points
                and weak equivalences the
                = succeed "wise" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stalk"} stalk
            %li#ModelStructureOnChainComplexesIsProper
              %p
                The standard
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structures+on+chain+complexes"} model structures on chain complexes
                (see
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "model+structure+on+chain+complexes#Properness"} there
            %li
              %p
                The projective
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+differential+graded-commutative+algebras"} model structure on differential graded-commutative algebras
                (unbounded). See
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/q/204414/381"} this MO discussion
          %h3#reedy_model_structures Reedy model structures
          %p
          #ReedyStructureInheritsProperness.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℛ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{R}
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+category"} Reedy category
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              which is left or right
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+model+category"} proper
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reedy+model+structure"} Reedy model structure
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Func
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℛ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Func(\mathcal{R}, \mathcal{C})
              is left or right
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+model+category"} proper
          This appears as
          = succeed "," do
            %a{:href => "#Hirschorn02"} Hirschorn (2002), Thm. 15.3.4 (2)
          = succeed "." do
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Kan"} Daniel Kan
          %h3#ProperEquivModels Proper Quillen equivalent model structures
          %p
            While some model categories fail to be proper, often there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalent
            one that does enjoy properness.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Every model category whose acyclic cofibrations are
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+algebraic+fibrant+objects"} model structure on algebraic fibrant objects
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Nikolaus10"}> Nikolaus 10, theorem 2.18
            )
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              be a simplicial (possibly multi-colored)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory"} theory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mi Alg
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T Alg
              be the corresponding category of simplicial T-algebras. This carries a model category structure where the fibrations and weak equivalences are those of the underlying simplicial sets in the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} classical model structure on simplicial sets
            %p
              Then there exists a morphism of simplicial theories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \to S
              such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the induced
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mi Alg
                        %mover
                          %mo ←
                          %mo →
                        %mi T
                        %mi Alg
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S Alg \stackrel{\to}{\leftarrow} T Alg
                  is a
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                        %mi Alg
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S Alg
                  is a proper
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
          %p
            This is the content of (
            %a{:href => "#Rezk02"}> Rezk 02
            )
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#general General
          %p
            The following says that left/right properness holds
            %em locally
            in every model category, namely between cofibrant/fibrant objects.
          #GoodPullbacksAndPushouts.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Given a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
                  of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
                  between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+objects"} cofibrant objects
                  along a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibration"} cofibration
                  is again a weak equivalence;
              %li
                %p
                  every
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                  of a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
                  between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant objects
                  along a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibration"} fibration
                  is again a weak equivalence.
          %p
            A proof is spelled out in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Hirschhorn"} Hirschhorn, prop. 13.1.2
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Reedy"} Reedy
          %p This gives a large class of examples of left/right proper model categories:
          %p
          #AllObjectsFibrantImpliesRightProper.num_cor
            %h6 Corollary
            %p
            %ul
              %li
                %p A model category in which all objects are cofibrant is left proper.
              %li
                %p A model category in which all objects are fibrant is right proper.
            %p
          %p
            See in the list of
            %a{:href => "#Examples"} Examples
            below for concrete examples.
          %p
            Notice that the prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GoodPullbacksAndPushouts"}
            applies only (in the right proper case, for concreteness) to pullbacks of fibrations along weak equivalences in which
            %em all three
            objects are fibrant, since a fibration with fibrant codomain also has fibrant domain. The definition of right proper, on the other hand, states this property in the case when
            %em none
            of the objects are assumed to be fibrant.
          %p
            One might consider as an “in-between” assumption the situation when only the common codomain of the fibration and the weak equivalence (hence also the domain of the fibration) are fibrant; but it turns out that this apparently-weaker assumption is sufficient to imply full right properness. This can be found, for instance, as Lemma 9.4 of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Bousfield01"} Bousfield 2001
          #FibrationsBetweenFibrantObjectsSuffice.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Suppose that in some model category, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
              is a fibration and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z\to Y
              a weak equivalence, with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              (hence also
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times_Y Z \to X
              is a weak equivalence. Then the model category is right proper, i.e. the same statement is true without the assumption that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is fibrant.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Suppose given
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ←
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y\leftarrow Z
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y
              is a fibration and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z\to Y
              a weak equivalence. Choose a fibrant replacement
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y\to R Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to Y \to R Y
              as a weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\to R X
              followed by a fibration
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R X \to R Y
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi Y
                      %mo ←
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R X \to R Y \leftarrow Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mi Y
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R X \times_{R Y} Y \to R X
              is a weak equivalence. By 2-out-of-3, the induced map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mi Y
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to R X \times_{R Y} Y
              is also a weak equivalence.
            %p
              Now by
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ken+Brown%27s+lemma"} Ken Brown's lemma
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z\to Y
              preserves weak equivalences between fibrant objects, and in particular preserves this weak equivalence
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mi Y
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to R X \times_{R Y} Y
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mi Y
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times_Y Z \to R X \times_{R Y} Z
              is a weak equivalence. However,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mi Y
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R X \times_{R Y} Z \to R X
              is a weak equivalence by the assumption, so by 2-out-of-3, the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %msub
                      %mo ×
                      %mi Y
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X\times_Y Z \to X
              is also a weak equivalence, as desired.
          %h3#HomotopyLimits Homotopy (co)limits in proper model categories
          #PullbackAlongFibrationsAreHomotopyPullbacks.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              In a left proper model category, ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushouts"} pushouts
              along cofibrations are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushouts"} homotopy pushouts
            %p
              Dually, in a right proper model category, ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullbacks"} pullbacks
              along fibrations are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullbacks"} homotopy pullbacks
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              This is stated for instance in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} HTT, prop A.2.4.4
              or in prop. 1.19 in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~clarkbar/complete.pdf"} Bar
            %p We demonstrate the first statement, the second is its direct formal dual.
            %p
              So consider a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    K &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; Y
                    \\\
                    \\big\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}} &amp;&amp; \big\downarrow
                    \\\
                    L &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; X
                    }
            %p
              in a left proper model category, where the morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \to L
              is a cofibration, as indicated. We need to exhibit a weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X' \stackrel{}{\to} X
              from an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
              that is manifestly a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushout"} homotopy pushout
              of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi L
                      %mo ←
                      %mi K
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \leftarrow K \to Y
            %p
              The standard procedure to produce this
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
              is to pass to a weakly equivalent diagram with the property that all objects are cofibrant and one of the morphisms is a cofibration. The ordinary pushout of that diagram is well known to be the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pushout"} homotopy pushout
            %p
              So pick a cofibrant replacement
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset \hookrightarrow K' \stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              and factor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K' \to K \to Y
              as a cofibration followed by a weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ′
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K' \hookrightarrow Y' \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} Y
              and similarly factor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mo →
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K' \to K \to L
              as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi L
                    %mo ′
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K' \hookrightarrow L' \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} L
            %p This yields a weak equivalence of diagrams
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                            %mo ′
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                            %mo ′
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ←
                              %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ′
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    Y &amp;\stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}&amp; Y'
                    \\\
                    \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}}
                    \\\
                    K &amp;\stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}&amp; K'
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}}
                    \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}}
                    \\\
                    L &amp;\stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}&amp; L'
                    }
                    \\,,
            %p where now the diagram on the right is cofibrant as a diagram, so that its ordinary pushout
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi L
                    %mo ′
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo ′
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X' \coloneqq L' \coprod_{K'} Y'
            %p
              is a homotopy colimit of the original diagram. To obtain the weak equivalence from there to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↖
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi W
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mo ↗
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi W
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ″
                            %mo ≔
                            %mi K
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mrow
                                %mi K
                                %mo ′
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ″
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mi K
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi W
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    K &amp;&amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp;&amp; Y
                    \\\
                    \&amp; \nwarrow^{\mathrlap{\in W}}
                    \&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \nearrow_{\mathrlap{\simeq}} &amp;
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; K' &amp;\to&amp; Y' &amp;&amp;
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}}
                    \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}}
                    \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp; L' &amp;\to&amp; X' &amp;&amp;
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{\in W}}
                    \\swarrow &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{\simeq}} &amp;
                    \\\
                    L'' \coloneqq K \coprod_{K'} L
                    \&amp;&amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp;&amp;
                    L'' \coprod_{K} Y
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in W}} &amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;&amp;  \downarrow
                    \\\
                    L &amp;&amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp;&amp; X
                    }
                    \\,,
            %p
              where the total outer diagram is the original pushout diagram. Here the cofibrations are as indicated by the above factorization and by their stability under pushouts, and the weak equivalences are as indicated by the above factorization and by the left properness of the model category. The weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ″
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L'' \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} L
              is by the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} 2-out-of-3 property
            %p This establishes in particular a weak equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi L
                    %mo ″
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi K
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X' \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} L'' \coprod_K Y
                    \\,.
            %p
              It remains to get a weak equivalence further to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ″
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ″
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mrow
                                %mi K
                                %mo ′
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi W
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    K &amp;\to&amp; Y
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    L'' &amp;\to&amp; L'' \coprod_{K'} Y
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in W}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    L &amp;\to&amp; X
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              Notice that the top square is a pushout by construction, and the total one by assumption. Therefore by the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pasting+law"} pasting law
            %p
              Then factor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \to Y
              as a cofibration followed by a weak equivalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Z
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K \hookrightarrow Z \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} Y
              and push that factorization through the double diagram, to obtain
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi K
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi cof
                          %mtd
                            %mi Z
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi W
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ″
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi cof
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ″
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mi K
                            %mi Z
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi W
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mo ″
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mrow
                                %mi K
                                %mo ′
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi W
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi W
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mi K
                            %mi Z
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi W
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    K &amp;\stackrel{\in cof}{\to}&amp; Z &amp;\stackrel{\in W}{\to}&amp; Y
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in \cof}}
                    \&amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}} &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    L'' &amp;\stackrel{\in cof}{\to}&amp;
                    L'' \coprod_{K} Z
                    \&amp;\stackrel{\in W}{\to}&amp;
                    L'' \coprod_{K'} Y
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in W}} &amp;&amp;
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in W}}
                    \&amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    L &amp; \to&amp; L \coprod_K Z &amp;\stackrel{\in W}{\to}&amp; X
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p Again by the behaviour of pushouts under pasting, every single square and composite rectangle in this diagram is a pushout. Using this, the cofibration and weak equivalence properties from before push through the diagram as indicated. This finally yields the desired weak equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ″
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                        %mo ′
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    L'' \coprod_{K'} Y \stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                    X
            %p
              by
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} 2-out-of-3
          %p
            If we had allowed ourselved to assume in addition that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            itself is already cofibrant, then the above statement has a much simpler proof, which we list just for fun, too.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_of_prop__assuming_that_the_domain_of_the_cofibration_is_cofibrant
              Proof of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PullbackAlongFibrationsAreHomotopyPullbacks"}
              assuming that the domain of the cofibration is cofibrant
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow B
              be a cofibration with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              cofibrant and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to C
              be any other morphism. Factor this morphism as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi C
                    %mo ′
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \hookrightarrow C' \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} C
              by a cofibration followed by an acyclic fibration. This give a weak equivalence of pushout diagrams
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo ≃
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↑
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    C' &amp;\stackrel{\simeq}{\to}&amp; C
                    \\\
                    \\uparrow &amp;&amp; \uparrow
                    \\\
                    A &amp;\stackrel{=}{\to}&amp; A
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    B &amp;\stackrel{=}{\to}&amp; B
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p
              In the diagram on the left all objects are cofibrant and one morphism is a cofibration, hence this is a cofibrant diagram and its ordinary colimit is the homotopy colimit. Using that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              diagrams compose to pushout diagrams, that cofibrations are preserved under pushout and that in a left proper model category weak equivalences are preserved under pushout along cofibrations, we find a weak equiovalence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hocolim
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ≃
                    %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi A
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hocolim \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} B \coprod_A C
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi cof
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                            %mo ′
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi W
                                %mo ∩
                                %mi fib
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %mo ∈
                                  %mi cof
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi hocolim
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mo ∈
                                %mi W
                          %mtd
                            %mi B
                            %munder
                              %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                              %mi A
                            %mi C
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A &amp;\stackrel{\in cof}{\to}&amp; C' &amp;\stackrel{\in W \cap fib}{\to}&amp; C
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}} &amp;&amp;
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}} &amp;&amp;
                    \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{\in cof}}
                    \\\
                    B &amp;\to&amp; hocolim &amp;\stackrel{\in W}{\to}&amp;
                    B \coprod_A C
                    }
                    \\,.
            %p The proof for the second statement is the precise formal dual.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              A model category is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+proper+model+category"} right proper
              if and only if every fibration is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+map"} sharp map
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Rezk98"}> Rezk 98
            )
          %h3#SliceCategories Slice categories
          %p
            For any model category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon A\to B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+categories"} slice categories
            along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⇄
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Sigma_f
                  \\;\colon\;
                  M/A
                  \\rightleftarrows
                  M/B
                  \\;\colon\;
                  f^*
          %p
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+model+categories"} slice model categories
            (
            %a{:href => "slice+model+structure#LeftBaseChangeQuillenAdjunction"}> this Prop.
            ).
          %p
            If this adjunction is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            must be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            are fibrant.
          %p
          #ViaSliceCategories.num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p The following are equivalent:
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                  is right proper.
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  is any weak equivalence in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi M
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Σ
                          %mi f
                        %mo ⊣
                        %msup
                          %mi f
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_f \dashv f^*
                  is a Quillen equivalence.
            %p
          %p
            This is due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Rezk02"} Rezk 02, Prop. 2.5
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The statement of Prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ViaSliceCategories"}
              may be read as saying
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is right proper iff all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+model+categories"} slice model categories
              have the “correct” Quillen equivalence type.
            %p
              Since whether or not a Quillen adjunction is a Quillen equivalence depends only on the classes of weak equivalences, not the fibrations and cofibrations, it follows that being right proper is really a property of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+category"} homotopical category
          %h3#local_cartesian_closure Local cartesian closure
          %p
            Since most well-behaved model categories are equivalent to a model category in which all objects are fibrant — namely, the model category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraically+fibrant+objects"} algebraically fibrant objects
            — they are in particular equivalent to one which is right proper. Thus, right properness by itself is not a property of an
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            However, if a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cisinski+model+category"} Cisinski model category
            is right proper, then the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} locally cartesian closed (∞,1)-category
            for the proof.
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-left-exact+left+Bousfield+localization"} semi-left-exact left Bousfield localization
          %h2#references References
          %p The concept originates in
          %ul
            %li#BousfieldFriedlander78
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aldridge+Bousfield"}> Aldridge Bousfield
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eric+Friedlander"} Eric Friedlander
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Homotopy theory of
                  = succeed "-spaces," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Γ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.rochester.edu/people/faculty/doug/otherpapers/bousfield-friedlander.pdf"} pdf
          %p Textbook account:
          %ul
            %li#Hirschhorn02
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Hirschhorn"}> Philip Hirschhorn
              , Chapter 13 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Model+Categories+and+Their+Localizations"} Model Categories and Their Localizations
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-99-s/"} ISBN:978-0-8218-4917-0
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.gbv.de/dms/goettingen/360115845.pdf"} pdf toc
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/hirschhornloc.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The usefulness of right properness for constructions of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy categories
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rick+Jardine"}> Rick Jardine
              ,
              %em Cocycle categories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/math/pdf/0605/0605198v1.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p The general theory can be found in
          %ul
            %li#Hirschhorn
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Hirschhorn"}> Philip Hirschhorn
              , chapter 13 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em Model Categories and Their Localizations
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/bookstore?fn=20&arg1=whatsnew&item=SURV-99"} AMS
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.gbv.de/dms/goettingen/360115845.pdf"} pdf toc
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/hirschhornloc.pdf"} pdf
          %p also in
          %ul
            %li#Reedy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chris+Reedy"}> Chris Reedy
              ,
              %em Homotopy theory of model categories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www-math.mit.edu/~psh/reedy.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Proposition
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FibrationsBetweenFibrantObjectsSuffice"}
            can be found in
          %ul
            %li#Bousfield01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aldridge+Bousfield"}> Aldridge Bousfield
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the telescopic homotopy theory of spaces
              %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/journals/tran/2001-353-06/S0002-9947-00-02649-0/"} web with fulltext
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li#Rezk98
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"}> Charles Rezk
                ,
                %em Fibrations and homotopy colimits of simplicial sheaves
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/9811038"}> arXiv:9811038
                )
            %li#Rezk02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Rezk"}> Charles Rezk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Every homotopy theory of simplicial algebras admits a proper model
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0166-8641(01)00057-8"} doi:10.1016/S0166-8641(01)00057-8
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0003065"} arXiv:math/0003065
            %li#Nikolaus10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Nikolaus"}> Thomas Nikolaus
                ,
                %em Algebraic models for higher categories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1003.1342"}> arXiv:1003.1342
                )
          %p Examples of nonproper model structures can be found in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Henry"}> Simon Henry
                ,
                %em Counter-example to the existence of left Bousfield localization of combinatorial model category
                %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/325383/counter-example-to-the-existence-of-left-bousfield-localization-of-combinatorial"} MathOverflow
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Henry"}> Simon Henry
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Examples of non-proper model structure
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/339084/examples-of-non-proper-model-structure"} MathOverflow
